ut_tokens": 5058}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-29T08:16:22.189Z", "request_id": "req_011CgWCKsiDPRqWH2ymKQzoY", "thinking_duration_ms": 42458}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 0}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 1, "content_block": {"type": "text", "text": ""}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgWCKsxr4Pb2dJmbJR2Ac", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I scored all eight plans with rough mental estimates, not a full day-by-day simulation, so these probabilities carry real uncertainty.\n\n**Unit economics behind the estimates**\n- **Product 0:** a lost sale costs 11 (9 margin plus a penalty of 2). A unit that goes to waste costs 8.\n- **Product 1:** a lost sale costs 11 (5 plus 6). A unit that goes to waste costs 9.\n- **What this means for order size:** both products should be ordered a little above average demand. Average demand is about 1.05 × forecast, so the right size is roughly 1.05–1.1 × forecast.\n- **Shared startup loss:** no plan can avoid shortages on days 1–4, because the first deliveries arrive only on day 3 (product 0) and day 5 (product 1).\n\n**Comparing the candidates**\n- **Candidates 4 and 5** order about 1.09 × forecast of product 0 and 1.17 × of product 1. That is close to the best level.\n  - Over the last few days, candidate 4 cuts orders below average demand. Near the end, leftover stock is worth nothing, but a shortage costs 11 while a wasted unit costs 8–9, so ordering slightly above average is still right.\n  - Candidate 5 orders at roughly average demand in those days, so I rate it slightly higher than 4.\n- **Candidate 6** orders about 1.06 × forecast. It also places one large first order (64 and 50). That stock carries over and costs a little in storage and waste, but it is a minor loss.\n- **Candidate 7** orders exactly at forecast (1.0 ×), which is below average demand. That causes regular shortages at about 11 per missing unit.\n- **Candidate 3** orders 1.16 × and 1.21 ×. Stock builds up steadily, so it pays for storage and writes off expired units. In my estimate its losses are about the same size as candidate 7's, so it is hard to say which of the two is worse.\n- **Candidate 2** orders every other day. That saves about 400 in daily order fees. But keeping a second day's worth of stock in the evening costs about 24 per day in storage, roughly 650–700 in total, so the net is about −300 against a good daily plan.\n- **Candidate 1** orders every three days. It saves about 560 in order fees but pays about 1,300 more in storage. Its order sizes are close to average demand, so it also runs short more often. I expect it to finish near the bottom.\n- **Candidate 0** orders 1.25–1.29 × forecast. That is far too much for products that expire, and I estimate it loses about 800–1,200 to waste and storage compared with candidate 4.\n\n**Estimated ranking:** 5 > 4 ≈ 6 > 3 ≈ 7 > 2 > 0 ≈ 1."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 5058, "cache_read_input_tokens": 886, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 5058}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-29T08:16:32.965Z", "request_id": "req_011CgWCKsiDPRqWH2ymKQzoY"}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 1}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"typ